.TH STATS 2
.SH NAME
stats: mean, variance, quantile, correlation, linefit, polyfit, multifit, normalcdf, tcdf, ttest1, ttest2, meanci \- statistics and regression
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "stats.m";
stats := load Stats Stats->PATH;
Line, Test: import stats;

stats->init();
m := stats->mean(x);
v := stats->variance(x);
(l, err) := stats->linefit(x, y);
(c, err) = stats->polyfit(x, y, 3);
(b, err) = stats->multifit(xs, y);
p := stats->normalcdf(z);
(t, err) := stats->ttest2(a, b);
(lo, hi, err) := stats->meanci(x, 0.95);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Stats
is descriptive statistics, least-squares regression, and the two
distributions a course needs.
.PP
.B VARIANCE
.B DIVIDES
.B BY
.IR n \-1,
and so does
.B stddev
and everything built on them.
That is the sample variance \- the estimate of the variance of whatever
the numbers came from \- and it is what is wanted nearly always.
.B Popvariance
and
.B popstddev
divide by
.IR n .
The difference is invisible for any
.I n
large enough to be interesting and glaring for any
.I n
small enough to check by hand, and no answer reveals which a library
chose, so it is said here.
.PP
.B Variance
is computed in two passes, from the mean, and not from the sum of
squares.
The one-pass formula subtracts two large nearly equal numbers: for five
values near a thousand million it returns exactly zero.
.SS Order statistics
.B Quantile
interpolates between the two neighbouring order statistics \- there are
nine definitions of this in the literature and they disagree; this is
the one R calls type 7 and is the usual default.
.B Median
is the half quantile.
.B Sorted
returns a sorted
.I copy
and leaves the caller's array alone.
.SS Regression
.B Linefit
fits a straight line and returns the slope and intercept, the
.IR r \u\s-22\s+2\d
that says how much of the variation the line accounts for, the standard
errors of both coefficients, and the residual standard deviation.
.PP
.B Polyfit
fits a polynomial of the given degree and returns its coefficients
.B highest
.B power
.BR first ,
matching
.IR control (2)
and the way a polynomial is written down.
.B Multifit
fits several predictors at once and returns the intercept followed by
one coefficient for each.
.B Rsquared
scores any set of predictions against the data.
.PP
Both go through the normal equations and
.IR linalg (2).
That is the textbook method, and it is what makes
.BR polyfit 's
answer the same as the one a student gets by hand.
It is also the numerically worse one: the normal equations square the
condition number, so a fit of degree much above 6 on data spanning a
wide range loses figures that a QR factorisation would keep.
.SS Distributions and tests
.B Normalcdf
is built from
.IR erfc ,
not from
.IR erf ,
so it keeps its figures far out in the tail: at \-10 it returns
7.6e\-24 rather than zero.
.B Normalquantile
brackets by bisection and finishes with Newton on the exact cdf, so it
is as good as
.I erfc
is rather than as good as some fitted approximation.
.PP
.B Tcdf
is Student's t, from the incomplete beta function.
.B Tquantile
inverts it by bisection.
.PP
.B Ttest1
tests a sample against a stated mean.
.B Ttest2
is
.BR Welch 's
test, which does
.B not
assume the two samples have the same variance \- the pooled test that
does is the one in the textbook and is the one that is wrong whenever
they differ.
Both return the statistic, the degrees of freedom and a two-sided
.IR p .
.B Meanci
is a confidence interval for the mean.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/stats.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR linalg (2),
.IR control (2),
.IR math-elem (2),
.IR statstest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The fitting and distribution functions return a diagnostic string, nil
on success: a line through points that all share one
.IR x ,
a polynomial with more coefficients than there are points, predictors
that are combinations of each other, a quantile at 0 or 1, a sample that
does not vary at all.
.SH BUGS
There is no chi-squared, no F distribution, and so no analysis of
variance and no test of a regression as a whole \- only of its
coefficients, through their standard errors.
.PP
There is no weighted regression and no way to fit without an intercept.
.PP
Nothing here is robust: one bad point moves a least-squares line as far
as it likes, and there is no median regression, no trimming and no
outlier test.
Anscombe's quartet is in
.IR statstest (1)
precisely because this library cannot tell its four data sets apart.
